<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Hall, D. G. E. (1981) A History of South-East Asia (4th ed.). London: The Macmillan Press Ltd.</li><li>Pigeaud, T. G. T. (1960) Java in the 14th Century: A Study in Cultural History, Volume III: Translations (3rd revised ed.). The Hague: Martinus Nijhoff.</li><li>Muljana, S. (2005) Menuju Puncak Kemegahan: Sejarah Kerajaan Majapahit. Yogyakarta: LKiS Pelangi Aksara.</li><li>Nugroho, I. D. (2011) Majapahit Peradaban Maritim. Suluh Nuswantara Bakti.</li><li>Prapanca, Mpu (2018) Kakawin Nagarakertagama: Teks Asli dan Terjemahan (2nd ed.). Yogyakarta: Narasi.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: Estimate: no count survives (Raden Wijaya gave a census record to the Yuan in 1293, but no figure from it is known); Java was &quot;populous exceedingly&quot; to Odoric of Pordenone and &quot;vast and densely populated&quot; to Chinese writers, and a standing army of 30,000, with levies besides, suggests a realm of some millions in eastern Java, Madura and Bali, without the overseas tributaries.</p></section>
