ns, learning the rites you may one day perform at court.","kid":"Priests of Shiva and of the Buddha led the kingdom’s ceremonies, and students learned from teachers in schools high in the hills.","rise":"Learning passed through the households of brahmana families and the Kadewaguruan, schools set in quiet forests or on hills and mountains far from habitation, each led by a mahāresi, a high priest also called dewaguru. Royalty too could enter religious life, as Gayatri Rajapatni did when she became a Buddhist nun.","why":"Religion had officials of its own.","people":[["mpu-prapanca","Mpu Prapanca"]]},{"n":5,"lv":4,"band":"specialists","name":"Professional soldiers","loc":"Prajurit","term":"Bhayangkara, wwang jaladhi","lbl":"c. 1–3% (est.)","w":2,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jong-ship.jpg","you":"You are a full-time soldier of the king, paid in gold and armoured in scales, and you may sail on a great jong to bring a rebellious vassal to heel.","kid":"Majapahit had a paid army of 30,000 soldiers and great ships called jongs that carried them across the sea.","rise":"A soldier could rise far: Gajah Mada led the Bhayangkara guard that saved Jayanegara in 1319 and became prime minister. Many died far from home in the kingdom’s wars: in southern Borneo the dead were burned at Tambak Wasi in 1358, and surviving soldiers, sailors and metalsmiths settled in Amuntai, Alabio and Nagara.","why":"The army had two kinds of men: prajurit, professional soldiers, and levies taken from the peasants.","people":[]},{"n":6,"lv":4,"band":"specialists","name":"Foreign merchants","term":"-","lbl":"c. 0.2–1% (est.)","w":0.6,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/majapahit-6.jpg","you":"You are a Muslim merchant settled in the port of Tuban, paying the special tax laid on foreigners who stay and do business in Java, while the spices of Maluku pass through your hands.","kid":"Traders from China, India, Arabia and nearby islands came to Majapahit’s ports to buy 